<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!-- Standalone parent for the qits-idp repo: the platform's own identity provider, for MACHINES
         and for PEOPLE. The machine half is an RS256 signing key that survives restarts, a JWKS, an
         OIDC discovery document, a `client_credentials` token endpoint and the commission API. The
         user half is accounts: a one-time register token, WebAuthn passkeys with an optional
         password, and opaque sessions the edge introspects. There is no OAuth dance for the
         first-party UI — the edge is the single ingress and terminates the browser session here.

         The qits-cd/qits-ci case exactly: DELIBERATELY ITS OWN GAV, no <parent>, versions that must
         stay in step with the platform (Quarkus, JDK release) duplicated on purpose — a clone of
         THIS repo alone must build, with no monorepo and no parent to inherit from. The directories
         are `idp/` and `service/`; the coordinates are namespaced (`eu.wohlben.qits:qits-idp-*`) so
         generic module names cannot collide in a shared ~/.m2.

         THE CLIENT EXISTS NOW, so Quinoa is here: qits-platform-spa-idp is a submodule at
         service/src/main/webui and Quinoa builds it during `package` and serves it at /idp/. That
         makes `./mvnw verify` need BOTH a node on PATH and an initialised submodule — `verify` runs
         `package` on its way to failsafe, and an empty webui/ stops it at "No package.json found in
         Web UI directory". `./mvnw test` still needs neither, because Quinoa is off in test mode.

         WHAT THE SIBLINGS HAVE AND THIS STILL DOES NOT: no Lombok, no MapStruct. There is no DTO
         layer worth a mapper — the whole output is two JSON documents, a JWT and a small commission
         API. Each comes back the day something here needs it, not before. -->
    <groupId>eu.wohlben.qits</groupId>
    <artifactId>qits-idp</artifactId>
    <version>2026.1003.95120</version>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>
    <name>qits-idp</name>

    <modules>
        <!-- Listed first: `service` depends on it. -->
        <module>idp</module>
        <module>service</module>
    </modules>

    <properties>
        <!-- THE PLATFORM'S OWN MAVEN REPOSITORY, and this repo's first two artifacts from it:
             qits-db-core (PatientPgDriver, which the idp datasource opens every connection through)
             and qits-arch-rules (the test that refuses to let the datasource baseline go missing).
             Both are published by qits-integrations-quarkus. The url is a property so the image
             build can point it at the address the builder container can actually reach — see
             docker/Dockerfile and .qits-maven-settings.xml. -->
        <qits.db-core.version>2026.1002.70617</qits.db-core.version>
        <qits.arch-rules.version>2026.1002.70617</qits.arch-rules.version>
        <!-- The user-story framework (test scope): this module's story catalogue —
             api/TokenIssuanceBootstrapIT plus the classes under api/../stories/ — is written as
             @UserStory methods, so a run that boots the packaged artifact also regenerates
             service/target/userstories/: the stories' documentation, network diagram included.
             Released out of libs/qits-userflows into the platform's own Maven repository, which is
             the same place the two jars above come from, so nothing new had to be arranged to
             resolve it.

             NO qits-service-mock BESIDE IT, unlike every sibling's copy of this block. A mock is
             what a service under test stands where its upstream is, and this service HAS none: it
             is the issuer, it validates nothing against an OIDC tenant, and the only host it dials
             is the postgres its own datasource names. So the catalogue registers no
             NetworkCapture.source at all — the shipped RestAssured tap is the whole diagram, every
             arrow points IN, and the one dependency this process does have arrives as a DECLARED
             jdbc edge in the two commissioning stories. -->
        <qits.userflows.version>2026.1002.72731</qits.userflows.version>
        <qits.maven.repository.url>https://registry.qits.wohlben.eu/artifacts/maven/maven</qits.maven.repository.url>
        <maven.compiler.release>25</maven.compiler.release>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <project.reporting.outputEncoding>UTF-8</project.reporting.outputEncoding>

        <quarkus.platform.artifact-id>quarkus-bom</quarkus.platform.artifact-id>
        <quarkus.platform.group-id>io.quarkus.platform</quarkus.platform.group-id>
        <quarkus.platform.version>3.34.6</quarkus.platform.version>

        <!-- Quinoa is a Quarkiverse extension: not in quarkus-bom, and its version does NOT track
             the platform's. 2.8.2 is built against Quarkus 3.27.3 LTS — older than the 3.34.6
             above, which is the direction that works, since an extension compiled against an older
             core runs on a newer one. 2.8.3 is built against 3.36.2 and would be an extension
             asking this core for API it may not have. Bump it only past a platform bump. Same pin
             as qits-platform-mirror and every other SPA-serving service. -->
        <quinoa.version>2.8.2</quinoa.version>

        <!-- Embedded PostgreSQL for the suite. The signing keys live in postgres now, so the tests
             that open the store have to run against one — and the first rule here is that a clone
             tests green with NO DOCKER, which rules out Testcontainers and Quarkus dev services
             alike. Zonky resolves real postgres binaries as ordinary Maven artifacts and spawns
             them as a child process: a maven dependency, not a container.

             Two coordinates, on purpose. The BOM pins which POSTGRES the binaries are (18.4.0 —
             the platform's own postgres tag), the library version is the harness around them, and
             they move independently. Every architecture the suite may run on is named explicitly
             in the module that uses it: the binaries are per-arch artifacts, and a missing one
             fails at test time rather than at resolution. -->
        <zonky.embedded-postgres.version>2.2.2</zonky.embedded-postgres.version>
        <zonky.postgres-binaries.version>18.4.0</zonky.postgres-binaries.version>

        <compiler-plugin.version>3.15.0</compiler-plugin.version>
        <surefire-plugin.version>3.5.4</surefire-plugin.version>
        <failsafe-plugin.version>3.5.4</failsafe-plugin.version>
        <jandex-plugin.version>3.4.0</jandex-plugin.version>

        <!-- `mvn verify` must be green on a clone with nothing installed. The packaged-artifact ITs
             run with -DskipITs=false, and `service`'s `native` profile flips it so a -Dnative
             build exercises the binary it just compiled instead of packaging it and asserting
             nothing.

             IT STAYS TRUE FOR THE USERFLOW RUN TOO, rather than the module opting itself back in
             the way qits-githost does. .config/qits/ci-event-userflows.yml wants exactly one of
             this module's two ITs — TokenIssuanceBootstrapIT, the userflow — and builds WITHOUT
             Quinoa, because the qits-platform-spa-idp submodule arrives EMPTY in a step container.
             A blanket -DskipITs=false would also run IdpPackagedSurfaceIT, half of which is about
             the client that run deliberately does not build (the base href, the deep links, the
             fallback that must not swallow a machine path), and would make a quinoa-less run red
             on a test that is right. The pipeline names the class instead:
             `-DskipITs=false "-Dit.test=TokenIssuanceBootstrapIT"`. Keeping this property's
             meaning ("run everything") intact is what makes that spelling readable. -->
        <skipITs>true</skipITs>

        <!-- Surefire's @{argLine} has no value unless something (jacoco/quarkus) sets it. -->
        <argLine/>
    </properties>

    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>${quarkus.platform.group-id}</groupId>
                <artifactId>${quarkus.platform.artifact-id}</artifactId>
                <version>${quarkus.platform.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <!-- Which postgres the embedded binaries ARE — 18.4.0, the tag the platform's own
                     qits-oci-postgresql image is built from, so the suite opens the same engine the
                     deployment does. Imported as a BOM because the per-arch artifacts are separate
                     coordinates. -->
                <groupId>io.zonky.test.postgres</groupId>
                <artifactId>embedded-postgres-binaries-bom</artifactId>
                <version>${zonky.postgres-binaries.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>io.zonky.test</groupId>
                <artifactId>embedded-postgres</artifactId>
                <version>${zonky.embedded-postgres.version}</version>
                <scope>test</scope>
            </dependency>
            <!-- The reactor's own module, so `service` depends on it version-less. -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
                <artifactId>qits-idp-domain</artifactId>
                <version>${project.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <!-- PatientPgDriver, named by the datasource's `jdbc.driver` line, and DbRetry,
                     which SigningKeys is compiled against. -->
                <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
                <artifactId>qits-db-core</artifactId>
                <version>${qits.db-core.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <!-- The platform's shared rules, test scope. DatasourceBaselineRules is what this
                     repo takes them for. -->
                <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
                <artifactId>qits-arch-rules</artifactId>
                <version>${qits.arch-rules.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <!-- The user-story framework, test scope at the use site. Same platform repository
                     as the two above. -->
                <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
                <artifactId>qits-userflows</artifactId>
                <version>${qits.userflows.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <!-- Managed here rather than versioned at the use site because Quinoa is in no BOM;
                     the pin and its reasoning stay in one place. -->
                <groupId>io.quarkiverse.quinoa</groupId>
                <artifactId>quarkus-quinoa</artifactId>
                <version>${quinoa.version}</version>
            </dependency>
        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>

    <repositories>
        <repository>
            <!-- Where the two qits jars above come from. Named `qits-maven` because the image
                 build's settings file mirrors exactly that id — see .qits-maven-settings.xml. The
                 host answers 401 anonymously: build with `-s .qits-maven-settings.xml` and the
                 QITS_MAVEN_* variables it reads, or give ~/.m2/settings.xml a `qits-maven` server. -->
            <id>qits-maven</id>
            <url>${qits.maven.repository.url}</url>
        </repository>
    </repositories>

    <build>
        <pluginManagement>
            <plugins>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>${quarkus.platform.group-id}</groupId>
                    <artifactId>quarkus-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${quarkus.platform.version}</version>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${compiler-plugin.version}</version>
                    <configuration>
                        <parameters>true</parameters>
                        <!-- The siblings disable incremental compilation because it leaves stale
                             generated classes behind; kept here so the modules behave alike. -->
                        <useIncrementalCompilation>false</useIncrementalCompilation>
                    </configuration>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${surefire-plugin.version}</version>
                    <configuration>
                        <systemPropertyVariables>
                            <java.util.logging.manager>org.jboss.logmanager.LogManager</java.util.logging.manager>
                        </systemPropertyVariables>
                        <!-- The platform injects QITS_DOMAIN into CI steps and agent containers; a
                             test JVM that inherits it picks up the live domain instead of the
                             suite's domain-less default. Keep it out. -->
                        <excludedEnvironmentVariables>
                            <excludedEnvironmentVariable>QITS_DOMAIN</excludedEnvironmentVariable>
                        </excludedEnvironmentVariables>
                    </configuration>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${failsafe-plugin.version}</version>
                    <configuration>
                        <!-- Same reasoning as the surefire plugin above: don't let the platform's
                             QITS_DOMAIN leak into the forked test JVM. -->
                        <excludedEnvironmentVariables>
                            <excludedEnvironmentVariable>QITS_DOMAIN</excludedEnvironmentVariable>
                        </excludedEnvironmentVariables>
                    </configuration>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>io.smallrye</groupId>
                    <artifactId>jandex-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${jandex-plugin.version}</version>
                    <executions>
                        <execution>
                            <id>make-index</id>
                            <goals>
                                <goal>jandex</goal>
                            </goals>
                        </execution>
                    </executions>
                </plugin>
            </plugins>
        </pluginManagement>
    </build>
</project>
